lesson:
When you write `Path b = a;` or `b = a;`, C++ copies the object. By default it copies **each member**. For an `int` or a `std::string` that is exactly right. For a raw pointer member it copies the **address**, so both objects now point at the same array. That is a **shallow copy**:

```
Path a(3);          // a.points -> [0 0 0]
Path b = a;         // b.points -> the SAME array
b.set(0, 9);        // a sees 9 too
// end of scope: ~Path runs twice on one array -> double delete, crash
```

A **deep copy** gives the new object its own array with the same values. You write that yourself with two special functions:

```
Path(const Path& other)                 // copy constructor: Path b = a;
    : points(new int[other.count]), count(other.count)
{
    for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) points[i] = other.points[i];
}
Path& operator=(const Path& other);     // copy assignment: b = a;
```

Copy assignment must also release the old array, and must survive `a = a` (self-assignment): make the new array **before** deleting the old one, or check `if (this == &other)`.

**The rule of three.** If a class needs a custom **destructor**, it almost certainly needs a custom **copy constructor** and **copy assignment** too. They come as a set because all three are about the same owned resource.

**The rule of five** adds the two **move** operations (`T(T&&)` and `T& operator=(T&&)`). Moving steals the resource and leaves the old object empty, which is far cheaper than copying an array. Mark them `noexcept` so `std::vector` uses them when it grows.

**Not copyable at all.** Some things should have one owner: an open audio channel, a network socket, a motor. Say so with `= delete`, and the compiler refuses every copy:

```
Channel(const Channel&) = delete;
Channel& operator=(const Channel&) = delete;
```

**The rule of zero: what professionals actually do.** Don't own raw resources in ordinary classes at all. Use members that already manage themselves: `std::vector`, `std::string`, `std::unique_ptr`, `std::shared_ptr`. Then you write **none** of the five, and copying, moving and destruction are all correct automatically. (A class with a `unique_ptr` member is automatically move-only, which is usually what you want.) Keep the rule-of-five code for the few small classes whose whole job is to wrap one resource.

In Unreal, `TArray` and `FString` are deep-copied like `std::vector`, which is why `const TArray<...>&` parameters matter: forgetting the `&` copies the whole array every call.

example.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

// rule of zero: no destructor, no copy functions, and copies are deep
struct Loadout
{
    std::string owner;
    std::vector<std::string> weapons;
};

int main()
{
    Loadout a{"Otieno", {"bow", "dagger"}};
    Loadout b = a;                 // a full, independent copy
    b.owner = "Wanjiru";
    b.weapons.push_back("staff");

    std::cout << a.owner << ": " << a.weapons.size() << " weapons\n";
    std::cout << b.owner << ": " << b.weapons.size() << " weapons\n";

    Loadout c = std::move(b);      // move: c takes b's vector, no copying
    std::cout << c.owner << " now has " << c.weapons.size() << ", b has " << b.weapons.size() << "\n";
    return 0;
}

